1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,950
Chapter 28: Singleton Breech Delivery.

3
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:20,387
Classification Diagnosis Delivery Route Labor And Delivery Partial Breech Extraction Total Breech Extraction External Cephalic Version References Classification Near term, the typical fetus spontaneously assumes a cephalic presentation.

4
00:00:20,387 --> 00:00:26,900
However, if the fetal buttocks or legs enter the pelvis before the head, the presentation is breech.

5
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:38,637
At term, breech presentation persists in 2 to 5 percent of singleton deliveries (Bin, 2016; Cammu, 2014; Toijonen, 2019).

6
00:00:38,637 --> 00:00:44,825
Breech delivery of a second twin is discussed in Chapter 48 (p. 856).

7
00:00:44,825 --> 00:00:53,912
The categories of frank, complete, and incomplete breech presentations differ in their varying relations between the lower extremities and buttocks.

8
00:00:53,912 --> 00:01:01,712
With a frank breech, lower extremities are flexed at the hips and extended at the knees, and thus the feet lie close to the head .

9
00:01:01,712 --> 00:01:08,837
With a complete breech, both hips are flexed, and one or both knees are also flexed (Fig. 28- 2).

10
00:01:08,837 --> 00:01:13,150
With an incomplete breech, one or both hips are extended.

11
00:01:13,150 --> 00:01:20,337
As a result, one or both feet or knees lie below the breech, and thus a foot or knee is lowermost in the birth canal.

12
00:01:20,337 --> 00:01:25,400
A footling breech is an incomplete breech with one or both feet below the breech.

13
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:27,912
Figure 28-1.

14
00:01:27,912 --> 00:01:30,400
Frank breech presentation.

15
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:32,837
Figure 28-2.

16
00:01:32,837 --> 00:01:35,412
Complete breech presentation.

17
00:01:35,412 --> 00:01:44,787
Of singleton term breech fetuses, the neck may be extremely hyperextended in perhaps 5 percent, and the term stargazing fetus is used .

18
00:01:44,787 --> 00:01:51,475
With transverse lie and similar hyperextension of the fetal neck, the term flying fetus is applied.

19
00:01:51,475 --> 00:02:01,575
With these, fetal or uterine anomalies may be more prevalent and are sought if not previously identified (Phelan, 1983; Shipp, 2000).

20
00:02:01,575 --> 00:02:06,875
With hyperextension, vaginal delivery can injure the cervical spinal cord.

21
00:02:06,875 --> 00:02:11,787
Thus, if identified at term, cesarean delivery is indicated .

22
00:02:11,787 --> 00:02:19,550
However, cases of spinal cord injury have been reported following uneventful cesarean delivery of breech fetuses.

23
00:02:19,550 --> 00:02:27,500
Here, the flexion itself may be implicated (Hernandez-Marti, 1984; Weinstein, 1983).

24
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:29,375
Diagnosis.

25
00:02:29,375 --> 00:02:31,762
# Risk Factors.

26
00:02:31,762 --> 00:02:38,100
Understanding the clinical settings that predispose to breech presentation can aid early recognition.

27
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:45,125
This fetal lie is more common remote from term, as earlier in pregnancy each fetal pole has similar bulk .

28
00:02:45,125 --> 00:02:47,987
Multifetal gestation is another .

29
00:02:47,987 --> 00:03:14,075
With singletons, other factors include extremes of amnionic fluid volume, fetal anomalies, structural uterine abnormalities, placenta previa, nulliparity, increased maternal age, female fetal gender, prior breech delivery, and size that is small for gestational age (Bin, 2016; Cammu, 2014; Noli, 2019; Roberts, 1999).

30
00:03:14,075 --> 00:03:24,950
One study found that following one breech delivery, the recurrence rate for a second breech presentation was 10 percent, and for a subsequent third breech it was 28 percent .

31
00:03:24,950 --> 00:03:27,325
# Examination.

32
00:03:27,325 --> 00:03:34,562
Leopold maneuvers to ascertain fetal presentation are discussed in Chapter 22 (p. 419).

33
00:03:34,562 --> 00:03:39,775
With the first maneuver, the hard, round fetal head occupies the fundus.

34
00:03:39,775 --> 00:03:47,387
The second maneuver identifies the hard, broad back to be on one side of the abdomen and the knobby small parts on the other.

35
00:03:47,387 --> 00:03:53,662
With the third maneuver, if not engaged, the softer breech is movable above the pelvic inlet.

36
00:03:53,662 --> 00:03:59,037
After engagement, the fourth maneuver shows the breech to be beneath the symphysis.

37
00:03:59,037 --> 00:04:06,362
The accuracy of this palpation varies (Lydon-Rochelle, 1993; Nassar, 2006).

38
00:04:06,362 --> 00:04:11,887
Thus, with unclear presentation, sonographic examination is indicated.

39
00:04:11,887 --> 00:04:21,850
During cervical examination with a frank breech, no feet are appreciated, but the fetal ischial tuberosities, sacrum, and anus are usually palpable.

40
00:04:21,850 --> 00:04:27,412
After further fetal descent, the external genitalia also may be distinguished.

41
00:04:27,412 --> 00:04:36,212
When labor is prolonged, the fetal buttocks may become markedly swollen, rendering digital differentiation of a face and breech difficult.

42
00:04:36,212 --> 00:04:43,350
In some cases, the anus may be mistaken for the mouth and the ischial tuberosities for the malar eminences.

43
00:04:43,350 --> 00:04:53,587
With careful examination, however, the finger encounters muscular resistance with the anus, whereas the bony, less yielding jaws and palate are felt through the mouth.

44
00:04:53,587 --> 00:04:57,562
The finger, upon removal from the anus, may be

45
00:04:57,724 --> 00:04:59,961
stained with meconium.

46
00:04:59,911 --> 00:05:07,636
The mouth and malar eminences form a triangular shape, whereas the ischial tuberosities and anus lie in a straight line.

47
00:05:07,636 --> 00:05:12,161
With a complete breech, the feet may be felt alongside the buttocks.

48
00:05:12,161 --> 00:05:17,311
In footling presentations, one or both feet are inferior to the buttocks.

49
00:05:17,311 --> 00:05:21,199
The fetal sacrum is palpated to establish position.

50
00:05:21,199 --> 00:05:29,511
As with cephalic presentations, fetal position is designated to reflect the relations of the fetal sacrum to the maternal pelvis.

51
00:05:29,511 --> 00:05:39,549
Thus, with left sacrum anterior (LSA), the fetus’s back is up and its sacrum occupies the left upper (ventral) quadrant of the mother’s pelvis.

52
00:05:39,549 --> 00:05:51,699
Other positions are right sacrum anterior (RSA), right or left sacrum posterior (RSP or LSP), and right or left sacrum transverse (RST or LST).

53
00:05:51,699 --> 00:05:53,636
Delivery Route.

54
00:05:53,636 --> 00:05:58,074
Multiple factors aid determination of the best delivery route.

55
00:05:58,074 --> 00:06:11,699
These include maternal parity and pelvic dimensions; coexistent pregnancy complications; provider experience; patient preference; hospital capabilities; and fetal size, anatomy, and gestational age .

56
00:06:11,699 --> 00:06:14,174
Table 28-1.

57
00:06:14,174 --> 00:06:25,636
Factors Favoring Cesarean Delivery of the Breech Fetus Compared with their term counterparts, preterm breech fetuses have distinct complications related to their small size and immaturity.

58
00:06:25,636 --> 00:06:31,224
Accordingly, a separate discussion of term and preterm breech fetuses is presented.

59
00:06:31,224 --> 00:06:33,886
# Term Breech Fetus.

60
00:06:33,886 --> 00:06:42,549
Current obstetrical thinking regarding vaginal delivery of the term breech fetus was tremendously influenced by results of the Term Breech Trial .

61
00:06:42,549 --> 00:06:50,524
This trial included 1041 women randomly assigned to planned cesarean and 1042 to planned vaginal delivery.

62
00:06:50,524 --> 00:06:59,786
These comparator groups reflect actual risks, and the planned vaginal delivery group included those who required intrapartum cesarean delivery.

63
00:06:59,786 --> 00:07:05,674
In the planned vaginal delivery group, 57 percent were actually delivered vaginally.

64
00:07:05,674 --> 00:07:16,711
Planned cesarean delivery was associated with a lower risk of perinatal mortality compared with planned vaginal delivery—3 per 1000 versus 13 per 1000.

65
00:07:16,711 --> 00:07:25,636
Cesarean delivery was also associated with a lower risk of “serious” neonatal morbidity—1.4 versus 3.8 percent.

66
00:07:25,636 --> 00:07:29,636
Short-term maternal morbidity was similar between groups.

67
00:07:29,636 --> 00:07:38,186
Critics of the Term Breech Trial emphasize that fewer than 10 percent of candidates underwent radiological assessment of pelvic capacity.

68
00:07:38,186 --> 00:07:47,236
In addition, most of the outcomes included in the “serious” neonatal morbidity composite did not actually portend long-term infant disability .

69
00:07:47,236 --> 00:07:54,649
Since that trial, however, additional data favoring cesarean delivery has come from the World Health Organization .

70
00:07:54,649 --> 00:08:07,086
In more than 100,000 deliveries from nine participating Asian countries, planned cesarean delivery offered improved perinatal outcomes for the term breech fetus compared with planned vaginal delivery.

71
00:08:07,086 --> 00:08:19,924
Other studies also have found lowered neonatal morbidity and mortality rates with cesarean delivery (Lyons, 2015; Rietberg, 2005; Vistad, 2015).

72
00:08:19,924 --> 00:08:32,411
From one metaanalysis, the calculated absolute risk of perinatal mortality was 0.3 percent, and risk of fetal birth trauma or neurological morbidity was 0.7 percent .

73
00:08:32,411 --> 00:08:38,186
In contrast, other studies support vaginal delivery as a suitable option at term .

74
00:08:38,186 --> 00:08:52,249
The Presentation et Mode d’Accouchement (PREMODA) study—which translates as presentation and mode of delivery —showed no differences in corrected neonatal mortality rates and neonatal outcomes according to delivery mode .

75
00:08:52,249 --> 00:08:56,074
Severe acute maternal morbidity scores were similar .

76
00:08:56,074 --> 00:09:02,911
This French prospective observational study involved more than 8000 women with term breech singletons.

77
00:09:02,911 --> 00:09:12,574
Strict criteria were used to select 2526 of these for planned vaginal delivery, and 71 percent of that group were delivered vaginally.

78
00:09:12,574 --> 00:09:25,299
Data from the Lille Breech Study Group in France also showed no excessive morbidity in term breech singletons delivered vaginally provided that strict fetal biometric and maternal pelvimetry parameters were applied .

79
00:09:25,299 --> 00:09:37,524
Other smaller studies support these findings as long as guidelines are part of the selection process (Alarab, 2004; Giuliani, 2002; Toivonen, 2012).

80
00:09:37,524 --> 00:09:40,961
Long-term evidence supporting vaginal breech

81
00:09:41,116 --> 00:09:45,341
delivery comes from Eide and associates (2005).

82
00:09:45,291 --> 00:09:55,816
These investigators analyzed intelligence testing scores of more than 8000 men delivered breech, and delivery route did not affect later intellectual performance.

83
00:09:55,816 --> 00:10:04,928
Moreover, a 2-year follow-up from the Term Breech trial showed similar risks for death and for neurodevelopmental delay between delivery groups .

84
00:10:04,928 --> 00:10:11,716
Other studies support these findings (Bin, 2017; Macharey, 2018).

85
00:10:11,716 --> 00:10:19,666
Despite evidence for this debate, at least in the United States, rates of planned vaginal delivery attempts continue to decline .

86
00:10:19,666 --> 00:10:28,141
Thus, as predicted, the number of skilled providers able to safely select and vaginally deliver breech fetuses steadily dwindles .

87
00:10:28,141 --> 00:10:34,516
Moreover, obvious medicolegal concerns make physician training in such deliveries difficult.

88
00:10:34,516 --> 00:10:46,216
In response, some institutions have developed birth simulators to improve resident competence in vaginal breech delivery (Deering, 2006; Maslovitz, 2007).

89
00:10:46,216 --> 00:10:53,941
In selecting suitable term breech vaginal delivery candidates, factors outlined in Table 28-1 are weighed.

90
00:10:53,941 --> 00:11:15,878
Of these, various national organizations recommend that the estimated term fetal weight lies between >2500 to 2800 g and <3800 to 4000 g (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2018b; Impey, 2017b; Kotaska, 2019).

91
00:11:15,878 --> 00:11:25,603
Fetal-growth restriction and oligohydramnios are other contraindications, because of their inherent association with poor neonatal outcome (Chaps.

92
00:11:25,603 --> 00:11:31,228
14 and 47, pp. 260 and 831).

93
00:11:31,228 --> 00:11:39,416
Data support this concern for the term breech fetus (Hinnenberg, 2019; Macharey, 2017a).

94
00:11:39,416 --> 00:11:45,516
Of uterine factors, prior hysterotomy carries an inherent risk for rupture in labor.

95
00:11:45,516 --> 00:11:57,353
The PREMODA, Term Breech Trial, and other studies allowed a trial of labor after cesarean for vaginal breech delivery, but in each, these constituted a small percentage of participants.

96
00:11:57,353 --> 00:12:02,041
Other studies have considered prior cesarean as a contraindication.

97
00:12:02,041 --> 00:12:11,278
Very limited data suggest a possible link with adverse perinatal outcome (Azria, 2012; Macharey, 2017a).

98
00:12:11,278 --> 00:12:20,316
Moreover, a prior cesarean delivery performed for arrested labor may suggest a relatively contracted pelvis and a greater labor risk.

99
00:12:20,316 --> 00:12:28,066
In contrast, parity and prior vaginal birth are positive factors and likely reflect sufficient pelvic capacity.

100
00:12:28,066 --> 00:12:34,803
Last, those with spontaneous labor and normal labor curves have higher successful vaginal birth rates.

101
00:12:34,803 --> 00:12:37,678
# Preterm Breech Fetus.

102
00:12:37,678 --> 00:12:45,366
In contrast to the term breech fetus, no randomized trials guide delivery selection for the preterm breech fetus.

103
00:12:45,366 --> 00:12:54,116
Comparison of the available observational studies is often hampered by their combining, splitting, or overlapping of gestational age groups.

104
00:12:54,116 --> 00:13:09,328
Other study biases include differing gestational age thresholds for planned neonatal resuscitation, assigning mortality causation to delivery rather than immaturity, and tendency to favor vaginal delivery for fetuses with a poor prognosis.

105
00:13:09,328 --> 00:13:20,403
In addition, the effects of precipitous vaginal delivery and associated incomplete antenatal prophylaxis with corticosteroids and antibiotics is difficult to evaluate.

106
00:13:20,403 --> 00:13:31,478
Despite these limitations, for preterm breech fetuses as whole, data support that planned cesarean delivery confers a survival advantage compared with planned vaginal delivery.

107
00:13:31,478 --> 00:13:39,378
Reddy and associates (2012) reported data from deliveries between 24 and 32 weeks’ gestation.

108
00:13:39,378 --> 00:13:52,453
For breech fetuses within these gestational ages, attempting vaginal delivery yielded a low success rate, and this group was associated with higher neonatal mortality rates compared with planned cesarean delivery.

109
00:13:52,453 --> 00:14:01,228
From a metaanalysis incorporating data from more than 3500 parturients, similar mortality associations were reported .

110
00:14:01,228 --> 00:14:15,591
In contrast, in their smaller cohort with gestational ages of 26 to 34 weeks, Lorthe and coworkers (2019) found similar rates of survival or of survival at discharge without severe morbidity.

111
00:14:15,591 --> 00:14:36,291
For less mature subgroups—23 to 28 weeks—the data are more conflicting as to whether survival rates are improved with planned cesarean delivery (Bergenhenegouwen, 2015; Grabovac, 2018; Kayem, 2015; Thomas, 2016; Tucker Edmonds, 2015).

112
00:14:36,291 --> 00:14:39,641
In the balance, cesarean delivery is

113
00:14:39,796 --> 00:14:46,921
proposed to avoid fetal injury from vaginal birth that may include anoxia, trauma, and head entrapment.

114
00:14:46,871 --> 00:14:57,258
This is measured against maternal morbidity from a classical uterine incision, which is often needed in this early preterm group due to a poorly developed lower uterine segment.

115
00:14:57,258 --> 00:15:12,108
This incision typically poses a risk of greater blood loss, higher infection rate, and increased risk of uterine rupture in future pregnancies (Patterson, 2002; Reddy, 2015; Sciscione, 2008).

116
00:15:12,108 --> 00:15:21,271
The high-level skills needed for safe vaginal delivery of the very preterm fetus and the ultimate prognosis of the neonate are other directing factors.

117
00:15:21,271 --> 00:15:40,408
For periviable fetuses, defined by them as 20 to 256/7 weeks, a consensus workshop of perinatal organizations concluded that “available data do not consistently support routine cesarean delivery to improve perinatal mortality or neurological outcomes for early preterm infants” .

118
00:15:40,408 --> 00:16:00,896
A subsequent joint statement by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2017) suggested consideration for cesarean delivery for periviable fetuses beginning at 230/7 weeks, with a recommendation for cesarean delivery at 250/7 weeks.

119
00:16:00,896 --> 00:16:10,121
For more mature preterm breech fetuses, that is, between 32 and 37 weeks, again limited data guide delivery route selection.

120
00:16:10,121 --> 00:16:20,458
Bergenhenegouwen and coworkers (2015) studied more than 6800 breech deliveries in a subgroup between 32 and 37 weeks.

121
00:16:20,458 --> 00:16:28,158
With planned cesarean delivery or planned vaginal birth, they found similar perinatal mortality and morbidity rates.

122
00:16:28,158 --> 00:16:30,946
All these findings shape practice.

123
00:16:30,946 --> 00:16:38,146
Thus, individualized decision-making is needed for the preterm breech fetus for whom resuscitation is planned.

124
00:16:38,146 --> 00:16:46,108
Provider skill, gestational age, parity, labor curve, and facility capability weigh heavily in this process.

125
00:16:46,108 --> 00:16:52,271
For the fetus in which resuscitation is not planned, vaginal delivery is likely preferable.

126
00:16:52,271 --> 00:16:55,183
# Delivery Complications.

127
00:16:55,183 --> 00:17:03,783
Compared with cephalic-presenting fetuses, higher rates of maternal and perinatal morbidity can be anticipated with breech presentations.

128
00:17:03,783 --> 00:17:10,833
For the mother, with either cesarean or vaginal delivery, genital tract laceration can be problematic.

129
00:17:10,833 --> 00:17:20,383
With cesarean delivery, added stretching of the lower uterine segment by forceps or by a poorly molded fetal head can extend hysterotomy incisions.

130
00:17:20,383 --> 00:17:35,596
With vaginal delivery, especially with a thinned lower uterine segment, delivery of the aftercoming head through an incompletely dilated cervix or application of forceps may cause vaginal wall or cervical lacerations, and even uterine rupture.

131
00:17:35,596 --> 00:17:44,871
Manipulations may also extend an episiotomy, create deep perineal tears, require cervical incision, and raise infection risks.

132
00:17:44,871 --> 00:17:56,083
If needed in select cases, inhalant anesthesia sufficient to induce appreciable uterine relaxation during vaginal delivery may cause uterine atony and postpartum hemorrhage.

133
00:17:56,083 --> 00:18:08,433
Maternal death is rare, but rates appear higher in those with planned cesarean delivery for breech presentation—a case-fatality rate of 0.47 maternal deaths per 1000 births .

134
00:18:08,433 --> 00:18:16,033
Other general short-term and long-term risks of cesarean delivery are listed in Chapter 30 (p. 548).

135
00:18:16,033 --> 00:18:22,596
For the fetus, prematurity and its complications are frequently comorbid with breech presentation.

136
00:18:22,596 --> 00:18:26,171
Rates of congenital anomalies also are greater .

137
00:18:26,171 --> 00:18:38,746
Compared with cephalic presentation, umbilical cord prolapse is more frequent with breech fetuses and with breech vaginal delivery (Behbehani, 2016; Critchlow, 1994).

138
00:18:38,746 --> 00:18:52,021
Collea (1980) reported that the incidence of prolapse approximated 0.5 percent with frank breech presentation, 5 percent with complete breech, and up to 15 percent with footling presentation.

139
00:18:52,021 --> 00:19:03,546
Rare fetal morbidity includes humeral or clavicular fracture, brachial plexus injury, and sternocleidomastoid muscle trauma (Chap. 33, p. 609.

140
00:19:03,546 --> 00:19:08,596
These may occur with either route but more frequently complicate vaginal birth.

141
00:19:08,596 --> 00:19:20,596
However, cesarean birth does not appear to afford greater protection against femur or skull fractures (Ekéus, 2019; Goffinet, 2006; Hannah, 2000).

142
00:19:20,596 --> 00:19:25,396
Rarely, traction may separate scapular, humeral, or femoral

143
00:19:25,564 --> 00:19:27,376
epiphyses .

144
00:19:27,326 --> 00:19:33,439
The spinal cord may be injured or vertebrae fractured, especially if great force is employed .

145
00:19:33,439 --> 00:19:37,426
Last, genitals may be injured with breech delivery .

146
00:19:37,426 --> 00:19:42,489
Some perinatal outcomes are inherent to the breech position rather than delivery.

147
00:19:42,489 --> 00:19:55,139
For example, development of hip dysplasia is more common in breech compared with cephalic presentation and is unaffected by delivery mode (de Hundt, 2012; Hinderaker, 1994).

148
00:19:55,139 --> 00:19:57,226
# Imaging.

149
00:19:57,226 --> 00:20:03,676
As noted, limited sonographic evaluation near term is reasonable to confirm presentation.

150
00:20:03,676 --> 00:20:10,601
Transabdominal sonography of the lower uterine segment in the sagittal plane shows the presenting fetal part.

151
00:20:10,601 --> 00:20:17,751
Prior to this and as part of prenatal care, sonographic fetal survey will have been performed in most cases.

152
00:20:17,751 --> 00:20:26,139
If not, gross fetal abnormalities, such as hydrocephaly or anencephaly, can be rapidly ascertained with sonography.

153
00:20:26,139 --> 00:20:31,026
This will identify many fetuses not suitable for vaginal delivery.

154
00:20:31,026 --> 00:20:39,901
It will also help to ensure that a cesarean delivery is not performed under emergency conditions for an anomalous fetus with no chance of survival.

155
00:20:39,901 --> 00:20:47,389
In many fetuses—especially those that are preterm or growth restricted— the breech is smaller than the aftercoming head.

156
00:20:47,389 --> 00:20:55,526
Moreover, unlike cephalic presentations, the head of a breech-presenting fetus does not undergo appreciable molding during labor.

157
00:20:55,526 --> 00:21:07,539
Thus, if vaginal delivery is considered, fetal size, breech type, and degree of neck flexion are evaluated (Fontenot, 1997; Rojansky, 1994).

158
00:21:07,539 --> 00:21:14,414
If needed, simple two-view radiography of the maternal abdomen also can define fetal head inclination.

159
00:21:14,414 --> 00:21:22,226
Sonographic identification of a nuchal arm or nuchal cord loops may warrant cesarean delivery to avoid neonatal harm .

160
00:21:22,226 --> 00:21:28,426
The accuracy of fetal weight estimation by sonography is not altered by breech presentation .

161
00:21:28,426 --> 00:21:34,576
For planned vaginal delivery at term, thresholds in Table 28-1 guide care.

162
00:21:34,576 --> 00:21:48,226
Moreover, a biparietal diameter (BPD) >90 to 100 mm is often considered exclusionary for vaginal delivery (Giuliani, 2002; Roman, 2008).

163
00:21:48,226 --> 00:22:00,001
Pelvimetry In addition to these requisite fetal parameters, some institutional protocols recommend pelvimetry to assess the maternal bony pelvis prior to planned vaginal term breech birth.

164
00:22:00,001 --> 00:22:10,376
Others, including our practice at Parkland Hospital, instead monitor the steady progression of fetal descent and cervical dilation to reflect adequate pelvic capacity.

165
00:22:10,376 --> 00:22:19,551
For pelvimetry, one-view computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance (MR) imaging, or plain film radiography is suitable.

166
00:22:19,551 --> 00:22:30,026
Comparative data among these modalities for pelvimetry are lacking, but CT is favored due to its accuracy, low radiation dose, and widespread availability .

167
00:22:30,026 --> 00:22:55,964
Although variable, some suggest specific measurements to permit a planned vaginal delivery: inlet anteroposterior diameter ≥10.5 cm; inlet transverse diameter ≥12.0 cm; and midpelvic interspinous distance ≥10.0 cm (Azria, 2012; Vendittelli, 2006).

168
00:22:55,964 --> 00:23:00,064
Some have recommended maternal-fetal biometry correlation.

169
00:23:00,064 --> 00:23:21,564
Appropriate values include: the sum of the inlet obstetrical conjugate minus the fetal BPD is ≥15 mm; the inlet transverse diameter minus the BPD is ≥25 mm; and the midpelvis interspinous distance minus the BPD is ≥0 mm .

170
00:23:21,564 --> 00:23:39,901
From small studies of MR imaging, vaginal delivery success rates approximating 75 percent were noted if the interspinous distance was >11 cm and true obstetrical conjugate was >12 cm (Hoffmann, 2016; Klemt, 2019).

171
00:23:39,901 --> 00:23:42,014
Labor And Delivery.

172
00:23:42,014 --> 00:23:44,551
# Labor Management.

173
00:23:44,551 --> 00:24:09,851
On arrival to the labor unit, surveillance of fetal heart rate and uterine contractions begins, and immediate recruitment of necessary staff includes: (1) a provider skilled in the art of breech extraction, (2) an associate to assist with the delivery, (3) anesthesia personnel who can ensure adequate analgesia or anesthesia when needed, and (4) an individual trained in newborn resuscitation.

174
00:24:09,851 --> 00:24:13,489
For the mother, intravenous access is obtained.

175
00:24:13,489 --> 00:24:23,401
This allows, if required, emergency induction of anesthesia or maternal resuscitation following hemorrhage from lacerations or from uterine atony.

176
00:24:23,401 --> 00:24:28,464
Assessing cervical dilation, membranes status, and presenting part

177
00:24:28,612 --> 00:24:31,487
station is essential for management.

178
00:24:31,437 --> 00:24:39,649
If labor is too far advanced, pelvimetry may be unsafe if fetal expulsion in the radiology department is a possibility.

179
00:24:39,649 --> 00:24:44,762
This alone, however, should not force the decision for cesarean delivery.

180
00:24:44,762 --> 00:24:50,687
Commonly, stepwise labor progression itself is a good indicator of pelvic adequacy .

181
00:24:50,687 --> 00:24:55,049
Sonographic assessment, described earlier, is completed.

182
00:24:55,049 --> 00:25:02,462
Ultimately, the choice of abdominal or vaginal delivery is guided by factors listed in Table 28-1.

183
00:25:02,462 --> 00:25:12,024
During labor, one-on-one nursing is ideal because of increased cord prolapse risks, and physicians must be readily available for such emergencies.

184
00:25:12,024 --> 00:25:16,049
Guidelines for monitoring the high-risk fetus are applied .

185
00:25:16,049 --> 00:25:21,862
For first-stage labor, most clinicians prefer continuous electronic monitoring.

186
00:25:21,862 --> 00:25:26,499
At minimum, the fetal heart rate is recorded every 15 minutes.

187
00:25:26,499 --> 00:25:32,162
A scalp electrode can be safely affixed to the buttock, but genitalia are avoided.

188
00:25:32,162 --> 00:25:39,337
When membranes rupture, either spontaneously or artificially, the cord prolapse risk rises appreciably.

189
00:25:39,337 --> 00:25:44,762
A small or preterm fetus or a non-frank breech presentation raises this risk.

190
00:25:44,762 --> 00:25:55,424
Therefore, vaginal examination is performed immediately following rupture, and special attention is directed to the fetal heart rate for the first 5 to 10 minutes thereafter.

191
00:25:55,424 --> 00:25:59,824
Continuous epidural analgesia is advocated by some.

192
00:25:59,824 --> 00:26:09,124
This may increase the need for labor augmentation and prolong second-stage labor (Chadha, 1992; Confino, 1985).

193
00:26:09,124 --> 00:26:18,987
These potential disadvantages are weighed against the advantages of better pain relief and increased pelvic relaxation should extensive fetal manipulation be required.

194
00:26:18,987 --> 00:26:26,362
Analgesia must be sufficient for episiotomy, for breech extraction, and for Piper forceps application.

195
00:26:26,362 --> 00:26:31,612
Nitrous oxide plus oxygen inhalation provides further relief from pain.

196
00:26:31,612 --> 00:26:36,187
If general anesthesia is required, it must be induced quickly.

197
00:26:36,187 --> 00:26:40,524
Normal second-stage labor should show progressive fetal descent.

198
00:26:40,524 --> 00:26:46,587
An initial passive second stage allows the breech to advance to the perineum and is recommended.

199
00:26:46,587 --> 00:26:55,912
In the PREMODA study, this stage lasted <30 min for 60 percent of parturients and >60 min for only 20 percent .

200
00:26:55,912 --> 00:27:07,149
For this passive phase, some recommend cesarean if the breech is not visible after 1½ to 2 hours (Impey, 2017b; Kotaska, 2019).

201
00:27:07,149 --> 00:27:11,112
Once the breech is visible, active pushing is encouraged.

202
00:27:11,112 --> 00:27:18,449
A hands-off approach in which hands are poised merely to support delivering parts is preferred to allow spontaneous delivery.

203
00:27:18,449 --> 00:27:26,424
In the PREMODA study, this active phase was <30 min for 95 percent of those with successful vaginal births .

204
00:27:26,424 --> 00:27:34,587
In their subanalysis of adverse neonatal outcome factors, an active second stage >20 min increased the risk .

205
00:27:34,587 --> 00:27:47,274
In the Term Breech Trial, the lowest risk for maternal and neonatal adverse outcomes was seen in the group with active second stages lasting <30 min (Su, 2003, 2007).

206
00:27:47,274 --> 00:27:54,324
In one small observational study, fetal adverse risks rose if this phase was >40 minutes .

207
00:27:54,324 --> 00:28:01,974
Canadian guidelines recommend cesarean if completed or imminent delivery is not accomplished after 60 min of pushing .

208
00:28:01,974 --> 00:28:05,449
# Labor Induction or Augmentation.

209
00:28:05,449 --> 00:28:10,999
Induction or augmentation of labor is controversial for term breech pregnancies.

210
00:28:10,999 --> 00:28:15,199
Here again, data are limited and mostly retrospective.

211
00:28:15,199 --> 00:28:24,599
With labor induction, Burgos and coworkers (2017) reported equivalent vaginal delivery rates compared with spontaneous labor.

212
00:28:24,599 --> 00:28:31,749
With induction, however, they reported higher rates of neonatal intensive care unit (NICU) admission.

213
00:28:31,749 --> 00:28:39,687
But, Marzouk and associates (2011) found perinatal outcomes and cesarean delivery rates not to differ.

214
00:28:39,687 --> 00:28:46,574
Last, others described greater cesarean delivery rates with induction but similar neonatal outcomes .

215
00:28:46,574 --> 00:28:55,537
In a metaanalysis of studies, the NICU admission rate was 3 percent with labor induction and was double that of those delivered from spontaneous labor.

216
00:28:55,537 --> 00:29:00,637
The cesarean delivery rate with induction was higher and was 33 percent .

217
00:29:00,637 --> 00:29:11,212
Compared with cephalic presentations, breech labor in general proceeds more slowly, but steady cervical progress is a positive indicator of adequate pelvic capacity .

218
00:29:11,212 --> 00:29:15,099
Thus, some protocols avoid augmentation for the

219
00:29:15,268 --> 00:29:25,055
breech-presenting fetus, whereas others recommend it only for hypotonic contractions (Alarab, 2004; Kotaska, 2019).

220
00:29:25,005 --> 00:29:36,755
In women with a viable fetus, at Parkland Hospital, we use amniotomy to promote contractions but prefer cesarean delivery instead of pharmacological labor induction or augmentation.

221
00:29:36,755 --> 00:29:39,780
# Vaginal Delivery Methods.

222
00:29:39,780 --> 00:29:44,193
Vaginal breech delivery is accomplished by one of three methods.

223
00:29:44,193 --> 00:29:52,743
With spontaneous breech delivery, the fetus is expelled entirely without any traction or manipulation other than support of the newborn.

224
00:29:52,743 --> 00:30:06,330
With partial breech extraction, the fetus is delivered spontaneously as far as the umbilicus, but the remainder of the body is delivered by provider traction and assisted maneuvers, with or without maternal expulsive efforts.

225
00:30:06,330 --> 00:30:11,930
With total breech extraction, the entire fetal body is extracted by the provider.

226
00:30:11,930 --> 00:30:15,155
# Spontaneous Breech Delivery.

227
00:30:15,155 --> 00:30:22,580
Similar to vertex delivery, spontaneous expulsion of a breech fetus entails sequential cardinal movements .

228
00:30:22,580 --> 00:30:30,843
First, engagement and descent of the breech usually take place with the bitrochanteric diameter in one of the oblique pelvic diameters.

229
00:30:30,843 --> 00:30:51,018
The anterior hip usually descends more rapidly than the posterior hip, and when the resistance of the pelvic floor is met, internal rotation of 45 degrees usually follows, bringing the anterior hip toward the pubic arch and allowing the bitrochanteric diameter to occupy the anteroposterior diameter of the pelvic outlet.

230
00:30:51,018 --> 00:30:59,543
After rotation, descent continues until the perineum is distended by the advancing breech, and the anterior hip appears at the vulva.

231
00:30:59,543 --> 00:31:11,393
By lateral flexion of the fetal body, the posterior hip then is forced over the perineum, which retracts over the fetal buttocks, thus allowing the fetus to straighten out when the anterior hip is born.

232
00:31:11,393 --> 00:31:16,893
The legs and feet follow the breech and may be born spontaneously or require aid.

233
00:31:16,893 --> 00:31:27,593
After the birth of the breech, there is slight external rotation, with the back turning anteriorly as the shoulders are brought into relation with one of the oblique diameters of the pelvis.

234
00:31:27,593 --> 00:31:36,343
The shoulders then descend rapidly and undergo internal rotation, with the bisacromial diameter occupying the anteroposterior plane.

235
00:31:36,343 --> 00:31:49,768
Immediately following the shoulders, the head, which is normally sharply flexed on the thorax, enters the pelvis in one of the oblique diameters and then rotates to bring the posterior portion of the neck under the symphysis pubis.

236
00:31:49,768 --> 00:31:52,455
The head is then born in flexion.

237
00:31:52,455 --> 00:31:58,668
Infrequently, rotation renders the fetal back to lie posteriorly instead of anteriorly.

238
00:31:58,668 --> 00:32:01,855
Such rotation is prevented if possible.

239
00:32:01,855 --> 00:32:10,980
Although the head can be delivered by allowing the chin and face to pass beneath the symphysis, the slightest traction on the body may cause extension of the head.

240
00:32:10,980 --> 00:32:15,605
With this, a longer diameter of the head must pass through the pelvis.

241
00:32:15,605 --> 00:32:18,518
# Partial Breech Extraction.

242
00:32:18,518 --> 00:32:24,005
With breech delivery, successively larger and less compressible parts are born.

243
00:32:24,005 --> 00:32:34,005
Thus, spontaneous expulsion is the exception, and vaginal delivery typically requires skilled provider participation for the fetus to navigate the birth canal.

244
00:32:34,005 --> 00:32:42,843
Noteworthy clinical pearls are provided by Yeomans (2017) in Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd edition.

245
00:32:42,843 --> 00:32:52,180
First, with all breech deliveries, unless the perineum is considerably lax, an episiotomy is made and is an important adjunct to delivery.

246
00:32:52,180 --> 00:33:02,905
As discussed in Chapter 27 (p. 510), mediolateral episiotomy may be preferred for its lower associated risk of anal sphincter lacerations.

247
00:33:02,905 --> 00:33:08,105
Ideally, the breech is allowed to deliver spontaneously to the umbilicus.

248
00:33:08,105 --> 00:33:13,230
Delivery of the breech draws the umbilicus and attached cord into the pelvis.

249
00:33:13,230 --> 00:33:24,155
Therefore, once the breech has passed beyond the vaginal introitus, the abdomen, thorax, arms, and head must be delivered promptly either spontaneously or assisted.

250
00:33:24,155 --> 00:33:32,980
The posterior hip will deliver, usually from the 6 o’clock position, and often with sufficient pressure to evoke passage of thick meconium.

251
00:33:32,980 --> 00:33:39,693
The anterior hip then delivers, followed by external rotation to a sacrum anterior position.

252
00:33:39,693 --> 00:33:44,168
The mother is encouraged to continue to push as the fetus descends

253
00:33:44,332 --> 00:33:46,869
until the legs are accessible.

254
00:33:46,819 --> 00:33:54,669
The legs are sequentially delivered by splinting the femur with the operator’s fingers positioned parallel to the long axis of the femur.

255
00:33:54,669 --> 00:33:57,507
This helps avoid femoral fracture.

256
00:33:57,507 --> 00:34:02,719
Pressure is exerted upward and laterally to sweep each leg away from the midline .

257
00:34:02,719 --> 00:34:05,219
Figure 28-3.

258
00:34:05,219 --> 00:34:12,219
To deliver the left leg, two fingers of the provider’s left hand are placed beneath and parallel to the femur.

259
00:34:12,219 --> 00:34:20,607
The thigh is then slightly abducted and pressure from the fingertips in the popliteal fossa should induce knee flexion and bring the foot within reach.

260
00:34:20,607 --> 00:34:25,769
The foot is then grasped to gently deliver the entire leg outside the vagina.

261
00:34:25,769 --> 00:34:28,919
A similar procedure is followed on the right.

262
00:34:28,919 --> 00:34:34,007
Reproduced with permission from Yeomans ER: Vaginal breech delivery.

263
00:34:34,007 --> 00:34:43,519
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

264
00:34:43,519 --> 00:34:48,007
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

265
00:34:48,007 --> 00:34:53,607
Following delivery of the legs, the fetal bony pelvis is grasped with both hands.

266
00:34:53,607 --> 00:34:59,444
The fingers should rest on the anterior superior iliac crests and the thumbs on the sacrum.

267
00:34:59,444 --> 00:35:04,082
This minimizes the chance of fetal abdominal softtissue injury .

268
00:35:04,082 --> 00:35:10,419
Maternal expulsive efforts are again used in conjunction with downward traction to affect delivery.

269
00:35:10,419 --> 00:35:13,069
Figure 28-4.

270
00:35:13,069 --> 00:35:21,882
To deliver the body, thumbs are placed over the sacrum, and each index finger wraps over the top of the corresponding fetal iliac crest.

271
00:35:21,882 --> 00:35:26,769
Gentle downward traction is applied until the scapulas are clearly visible.

272
00:35:26,769 --> 00:35:31,857
Reproduced with permission from Yeomans ER: Vaginal breech delivery.

273
00:35:31,857 --> 00:35:41,369
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

274
00:35:41,369 --> 00:35:45,857
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

275
00:35:45,857 --> 00:35:55,069
A cardinal rule in successful breech extraction is to employ steady, gentle, downward traction until the lower halves of the scapulas are delivered.

276
00:35:55,069 --> 00:36:01,032
Subsequent delivery of the shoulders and arms is not attempted until one axilla becomes visible.

277
00:36:01,032 --> 00:36:07,319
It makes little difference which shoulder is delivered first, and two methods are suitable for their delivery.

278
00:36:07,319 --> 00:36:16,819
In the first method, with the scapulas visible, the trunk is rotated either clockwise or counterclockwise to bring the anterior shoulder and arm into view .

279
00:36:16,819 --> 00:36:23,407
During delivery of the arm, fingers and hand are aligned parallel to the humerus and act as a splint.

280
00:36:23,407 --> 00:36:26,869
Perpendicular force risks humeral fracture.

281
00:36:26,869 --> 00:36:35,469
The body of the fetus is then rotated 180 degrees in the reverse direction to bring the other shoulder and arm into position for delivery.

282
00:36:35,469 --> 00:36:38,144
Figure 28-5.

283
00:36:38,144 --> 00:36:48,232
A. After delivery of the first arm, 180-degree rotation of the fetal body brings the sacrum to a right sacrum transverse (RST) position.

284
00:36:48,232 --> 00:36:54,119
B. Fingers of the provider’s hand extended over the right shoulder and parallel to the humerus.

285
00:36:54,119 --> 00:36:57,807
These sweep the arm downward across the chest and out.

286
00:36:57,807 --> 00:37:02,894
Reproduced with permission from Yeomans ER: Vaginal breech delivery.

287
00:37:02,894 --> 00:37:12,407
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

288
00:37:12,407 --> 00:37:16,894
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

289
00:37:16,894 --> 00:37:21,307
The second method is employed if trunk rotation is unsuccessful.

290
00:37:21,307 --> 00:37:25,744
With this maneuver, the posterior shoulder is delivered first.

291
00:37:25,744 --> 00:37:31,494
For this, the feet are grasped in one hand and drawn upward over the inner thigh of the mother .

292
00:37:31,494 --> 00:37:41,207
The operator’s hand enters over the fetal shoulder, fingers are aligned parallel to the long axis of the fetal humerus, and the fetal arm is swept upward.

293
00:37:41,207 --> 00:37:47,569
The posterior shoulder slides out over the perineal margin and is usually followed by the arm and hand.

294
00:37:47,569 --> 00:37:57,094
Then, by depressing the body of the fetus, the anterior shoulder emerges beneath the pubic arch, and the arm and hand usually follow spontaneously.

295
00:37:57,094 --> 00:38:03,832
After both shoulders are delivered, the back of the fetus tends to rotate spontaneously to the symphysis.

296
00:38:03,832 --> 00:38:07,057
Delivery of the head may then be accomplished.

297
00:38:07,057 --> 00:38:09,732
Figure 28-6.

298
00:38:09,732 --> 00:38:14,094
Infrequently, the posterior arm must be delivered first.

299
00:38:14,094 --> 00:38:19,694
For this, the lower half of the fetal body is raised up and over the maternal groin.

300
00:38:19,694 --> 00:38:25,369
The provider’s fingers are inserted under the posterior shoulder and aligned with the humerus.

301
00:38:25,369 --> 00:38:28,432
Reproduced with permission from Yeomans

302
00:38:28,588 --> 00:38:31,525
ER: Vaginal breech delivery.

303
00:38:31,475 --> 00:38:40,988
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

304
00:38:40,988 --> 00:38:45,475
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

305
00:38:45,475 --> 00:38:53,863
Nuchal Arm During delivery, one or both fetal arms occasionally may lie across the back of the neck and become trapped at the pelvic inlet.

306
00:38:53,863 --> 00:39:05,625
With such a nuchal arm, delivery is more difficult and can be aided by rotating the fetus through a half circle in such a direction that the friction exerted by the birth canal will draw the elbow toward the face .

307
00:39:05,625 --> 00:39:13,325
With a right nuchal arm, the body should be rotated counterclockwise, which rotates the fetal back toward the maternal right.

308
00:39:13,325 --> 00:39:20,300
With a left nuchal arm, the rotation is clockwise, which rotates the fetal back toward the maternal left.

309
00:39:20,300 --> 00:39:28,025
If rotation fails to free the nuchal arm, it may be necessary to push the fetus upward to a roomier part of the pelvis.

310
00:39:28,025 --> 00:39:35,300
If the rotation is still unsuccessful, still in this roomier part of the pelvic, the nuchal arm can be extracted.

311
00:39:35,300 --> 00:39:44,013
For this, the operator’s hand enters over the fetal shoulder, and fingers are aligned parallel to the long axis of the fetal humerus.

312
00:39:44,013 --> 00:39:51,650
Downward pressure flexes the elbow, and the fetal arm is then swept forward and down the ventral body surface for delivery.

313
00:39:51,650 --> 00:39:56,125
In this event, fracture of the humerus or clavicle is common.

314
00:39:56,125 --> 00:39:58,788
Figure 28-7.

315
00:39:58,788 --> 00:40:08,875
Reduction of a right nuchal arm is accomplished by rotating the fetal body 180 degrees counterclockwise, which directs the fetal back to the maternal right.

316
00:40:08,875 --> 00:40:13,475
Friction exerted by the birth canal will draw the elbow toward the face.

317
00:40:13,475 --> 00:40:19,825
Delivery of the Aftercoming Head The head of the fetus is normally extracted with one of the three maneuvers.

318
00:40:19,825 --> 00:40:24,250
With any of these, hyperextension of the fetal neck is avoided.

319
00:40:24,250 --> 00:40:35,900
First, with the Mauriceau maneuver, the index and middle finger of one hand are applied over the maxilla, to flex the head, while the fetal body rests on the palm of the same hand and forearm .

320
00:40:35,900 --> 00:40:38,663
Fetal legs straddle the forearm.

321
00:40:38,663 --> 00:40:42,875
Two fingers of the other hand hook over and grasp the shoulders.

322
00:40:42,875 --> 00:40:49,113
Downward traction is concurrently applied until the suboccipital region appears under the symphysis.

323
00:40:49,113 --> 00:40:55,263
Gentle suprapubic pressure simultaneously applied by an assistant helps keep the head flexed.

324
00:40:55,263 --> 00:41:04,850
The body is slightly elevated toward the maternal abdomen, and the mouth, nose, brow, and eventually the occiput emerge successively over the perineum.

325
00:41:04,850 --> 00:41:17,150
With this maneuver, the provider uses both hands simultaneously to exert continuous downward gentle traction while balancing forces between the fetal neck and maxilla to avoid neck hyperextension.

326
00:41:17,150 --> 00:41:19,525
Figure 28-8.

327
00:41:19,525 --> 00:41:23,975
A. Delivery of the aftercoming head using the Mauriceau maneuver.

328
00:41:23,975 --> 00:41:31,875
Note that as the fetal head is being delivered, flexion of the head is maintained by suprapubic pressure provided by an assistant.

329
00:41:31,875 --> 00:41:39,263
B. Pressure on the maxilla is applied simultaneously by the operator as upward and outward traction is exerted.

330
00:41:39,263 --> 00:41:44,563
Second, specialized forceps can be used to deliver the aftercoming head.

331
00:41:44,563 --> 00:41:54,613
Piper forceps, shown in Figure 28-9, or Laufe-Piper forceps may be applied electively or when the Mauriceau maneuver cannot be accomplished easily.

332
00:41:54,613 --> 00:42:04,800
The blades of the forceps are not applied to the aftercoming head until it has been brought into the pelvis by gentle traction, combined with suprapubic pressure, and is engaged.

333
00:42:04,800 --> 00:42:13,975
Suspension of the body of the fetus in a towel effectively holds the fetus up and helps keep the arms and cord out of the way as the forceps blades are applied.

334
00:42:13,975 --> 00:42:16,550
Figure 28-9.

335
00:42:16,550 --> 00:42:20,338
Piper forceps for delivery of the aftercoming head.

336
00:42:20,338 --> 00:42:28,388
Because the forceps blades are directed upward from the level of the perineum, some choose to apply them from a one-knee kneeling position.

337
00:42:28,388 --> 00:42:34,538
Piper forceps have a downward arch in the shank to accommodate the fetal body and lack a pelvic curve.

338
00:42:34,538 --> 00:42:42,575
This shape permits direct application of the cephalic curve of the blade along the length of the maternal vagina and fetal parietal bone.

339
00:42:42,575 --> 00:42:47,363
The blade to be placed on the maternal left is held in the provider’s left hand.

340
00:42:47,363 --> 00:42:53,888
The right hand slides between the fetal head and left maternal vaginal sidewall to guide the blade inward

341
00:42:54,052 --> 00:42:56,627
and around the parietal bone.

342
00:42:56,577 --> 00:42:59,814
The opposite blade mirrors this application.

343
00:42:59,814 --> 00:43:05,614
Once in place, the blades are articulated, and the fetal body rests across the shanks.

344
00:43:05,614 --> 00:43:11,514
The head is delivered by pulling gently outward and slightly raising the handle simultaneously.

345
00:43:11,514 --> 00:43:18,914
This rolls the face over the perineum, while the occiput remains beneath the symphysis until after the brow delivers.

346
00:43:18,914 --> 00:43:23,902
Ideally, the head and body move in unison to minimize neck extension.

347
00:43:23,902 --> 00:43:29,502
Last, in some cases, the back of the fetus fails to rotate to the symphysis.

348
00:43:29,502 --> 00:43:33,914
The fetus still may be delivered using the modified Prague maneuver.

349
00:43:33,914 --> 00:43:43,039
With this, two fingers of one hand grasp the shoulders of the back-down fetus from below while the other hand draws the feet up and over the maternal abdomen .

350
00:43:43,039 --> 00:43:45,539
Figure 28-10.

351
00:43:45,539 --> 00:43:53,939
Delivery of the aftercoming head using the modified Prague maneuver necessitated by failure of the fetal trunk to rotate anteriorly.

352
00:43:53,939 --> 00:44:00,639
Head Entrapment Either an incompletely dilated cervix or cephalopelvic disproportion can entrap the head.

353
00:44:00,639 --> 00:44:06,827
At this point, a true emergency exists because significant cord compression must be assumed.

354
00:44:06,827 --> 00:44:14,177
First, an incompletely dilated cervix can constrict around the neck, especially with a small preterm fetus.

355
00:44:14,177 --> 00:44:20,739
With gentle traction on the fetal body, the cervix at times may be manually slipped over the occiput.

356
00:44:20,739 --> 00:44:24,727
If unsuccessful, Dührssen incisions may be needed .

357
00:44:24,727 --> 00:44:31,677
General anesthesia with halogenated agents is another option to aid lower uterine segment relaxation.

358
00:44:31,677 --> 00:44:41,389
As an extreme measure, replacement of the fetus higher into the vagina and uterus, followed by cesarean delivery, can rescue an entrapped breech fetus.

359
00:44:41,389 --> 00:44:47,214
This Zavanelli maneuver is classically performed to relieve intractable shoulder dystocia .

360
00:44:47,214 --> 00:44:57,027
However, case reports have also described its use for an entrapped aftercoming head (Sandberg, 1999; Steyn, 1994).

361
00:44:57,027 --> 00:44:59,764
Figure 28-11.

362
00:44:59,764 --> 00:45:06,439
Dührssen incision being cut at 2 o’clock, which is followed by a second incision if needed at 10 o’clock.

363
00:45:06,439 --> 00:45:11,177
Infrequently, an additional incision is required at 6 o’clock.

364
00:45:11,177 --> 00:45:18,389
The incisions are so placed as to minimize bleeding from the laterally located cervical branches of the uterine artery.

365
00:45:18,389 --> 00:45:24,977
After delivery, the incisions are repaired as described in Chapter 42 (p. 740).

366
00:45:24,977 --> 00:45:38,327
Second, in cases with cephalopelvic disproportion and arrest of aftercoming head, the Zavanelli maneuver or symphysiotomy are options (Sunday-Adeoye, 2004; Wery, 2013).

367
00:45:38,327 --> 00:45:42,877
These are described in Chapter 27 (p. 504).

368
00:45:42,877 --> 00:45:45,752
# Total Breech Extraction.

369
00:45:45,752 --> 00:45:55,352
Frank Breech During complete extraction of a frank breech, moderate traction is exerted by a finger in each groin and aided by a generous episiotomy.

370
00:45:55,352 --> 00:46:02,139
Once the breech is pulled through the introitus, the steps just described for partial breech extraction are completed.

371
00:46:02,139 --> 00:46:09,139
Rarely during vaginal delivery, a frank breech will require decomposition inside the uterine cavity.

372
00:46:09,139 --> 00:46:16,039
Attributed to Pinard (1889), this procedure converts a frank breech into a footling breech.

373
00:46:16,039 --> 00:46:20,902
It is accomplished more readily if the membranes have ruptured only recently.

374
00:46:20,902 --> 00:46:27,927
It becomes extremely difficult if amnionic fluid is scant and the uterus is tightly contracted around the fetus.

375
00:46:27,927 --> 00:46:37,889
Pharmacological relaxation by general anesthesia or intravenous magnesium sulfate, nitroglycerin, or a betamimetic agent may be required.

376
00:46:37,889 --> 00:46:46,902
To begin, two fingers are carried up along one leg to externally rotate the hip by pressing on the medial side of the thigh parallel to the femur.

377
00:46:46,902 --> 00:46:57,164
Simultaneously, pressure in the popliteal fossa should prompt spontaneous knee flexion which brings the corresponding foot into contact with the back of the provider’s hand.

378
00:46:57,164 --> 00:47:00,714
The fetal foot then may be grasped and brought down.

379
00:47:00,714 --> 00:47:05,764
Delivery is then accomplished as for incomplete breech, described next.

380
00:47:05,764 --> 00:47:15,377
Incomplete or Complete Breech If rapid labor progress prohibits cesarean delivery, total extraction of an incomplete or complete breech may be required.

381
00:47:15,377 --> 00:47:20,327
A hand is introduced through the vagina, and both fetal feet are grasped.

382
00:47:20,327 --> 00:47:24,139
The ankles are held with the middle finger lying between them.

383
00:47:24,139 --> 00:47:28,202
With gentle traction, the feet are brought through the introitus .

384
00:47:28,202 --> 00:47:31,364
As the legs begin to emerge through the vulva,

385
00:47:31,516 --> 00:47:34,541
downward gentle traction is continued.

386
00:47:34,491 --> 00:47:39,041
As the legs emerge, successively higher portions are grasped.

387
00:47:39,041 --> 00:47:45,128
When the breech appears at the vaginal outlet, gentle traction is applied until the hips are delivered.

388
00:47:45,128 --> 00:47:50,216
The thumbs are then placed over the sacrum and the fingers over the iliac crests.

389
00:47:50,216 --> 00:47:55,478
Breech extraction is then completed, as described for partial breech extraction.

390
00:47:55,478 --> 00:47:58,041
Figure 28-12.

391
00:47:58,041 --> 00:48:02,616
Complete breech extraction begins with traction on the feet and ankles.

392
00:48:02,616 --> 00:48:12,216
If only one foot can be grasped, it can be brought down into the vagina and held with the appropriate hand, right hand for right foot and left hand for left foot .

393
00:48:12,216 --> 00:48:20,178
With the first foot secure, the opposite hand is introduced, passed upward along the leg, and guided to locate the other foot.

394
00:48:20,178 --> 00:48:26,253
If the remaining hip is extended, the second foot is usually easily grasped and brought down.

395
00:48:26,253 --> 00:48:35,566
If the hip is flexed and knee extended, a finger is hooked into that groin, and traction will bring the lower half of the fetus down until the leg can be reached.

396
00:48:35,566 --> 00:48:41,178
For cesarean delivery, these same total breech extraction maneuvers can be used.

397
00:48:41,178 --> 00:48:47,916
These steps can aid delivery of a frank, complete, or incomplete breech through the hysterotomy incision.

398
00:48:47,916 --> 00:48:50,478
External Cephalic Version.

399
00:48:50,478 --> 00:49:02,791
With version, fetal presentation is altered by physically substituting one pole of a longitudinal presentation for the other, or converting an oblique or transverse lie into a longitudinal presentation.

400
00:49:02,791 --> 00:49:10,216
Manipulations performed through the abdominal wall that yield a cephalic presentation are termed external cephalic version.

401
00:49:10,216 --> 00:49:18,203
Manipulations accomplished inside the uterine cavity that yield a breech presentation are designated internal podalic version.

402
00:49:18,203 --> 00:49:26,091
This latter procedure is reserved for delivery of a second twin and described in Chapter 48 (p. 856).

403
00:49:26,091 --> 00:49:28,491
# Indications.

404
00:49:28,491 --> 00:49:41,891
External cephalic version (ECV) reduces the rate of noncephalic presentation at birth, and its success rate is 50 to 60 percent (Hofmeyr, 2015b; Melo, 2019).

405
00:49:41,891 --> 00:49:47,478
For women with a transverse lie, the overall success rate is significantly higher.

406
00:49:47,478 --> 00:50:00,891
In general, ECV is attempted before labor in a woman who has reached 370/7 weeks’ gestation (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020; Impey, 2017a).

407
00:50:00,891 --> 00:50:10,303
This threshold aims to balance risks of fetal immaturity and the greater amnionic fluid volume seen in early-term pregnancies, which aids turning.

408
00:50:10,303 --> 00:50:23,928
In support of this, one systematic review found that ECV done before 370/7 weeks raised ECV success rates but did not lower the ultimate cesarean delivery rate and increased the risk of latepreterm birth .

409
00:50:23,928 --> 00:50:30,691
Before this time, breech presentation also still has a high likelihood of correcting spontaneously.

410
00:50:30,691 --> 00:50:36,628
And, if ECV is performed too early, time may allow a reversion back to breech .

411
00:50:36,628 --> 00:50:45,816
Last, if attempts at version cause a need for immediate delivery, complications of iatrogenic early-term delivery generally are not severe.

412
00:50:45,816 --> 00:50:49,703
Absolute contraindications to ECV are few.

413
00:50:49,703 --> 00:50:55,841
It is contraindicated if vaginal delivery is not an option, such as with placenta previa.

414
00:50:55,841 --> 00:51:11,041
Relative contraindications are early labor, oligohydramnios or ruptured membranes, known nuchal cord, structural uterine abnormalities, fetal-growth restriction, multifetal gestation, and prior abruption or its risk factors .

415
00:51:11,041 --> 00:51:16,453
Little data guide the decision for ECV for women with a prior cesarean delivery.

416
00:51:16,453 --> 00:51:23,716
In one pooled series of small studies that contained more than 100 attempts, no uterine ruptures were reported .

417
00:51:23,716 --> 00:51:28,216
At Parkland Hospital, we do not attempt ECV in these women.

418
00:51:28,216 --> 00:51:32,628
Several factors can improve the chances of an ECV attempt.

419
00:51:32,628 --> 00:51:45,991
These are multiparity, unengaged presenting part, nonanterior placenta, nonobese patient, and abundant amnionic fluid (Lauterbach, 2021; Kok, 2008, 2009).

420
00:51:45,991 --> 00:51:59,328
To augment the last parameter, neither a preprocedural 2-L intravenous fluid bolus nor amnioinfusion raised ECV success rates (Burgos, 2014; Diguisto, 2018).

421
00:51:59,328 --> 00:52:06,653
Dedicated breech teams may increase these rates (Hickland, 2018; Thissen, 2019).

422
00:52:06,653 --> 00:52:09,128
# Complications.

423
00:52:09,128 --> 00:52:17,853
Patient counseling includes a discussion regarding small but real risks for placental abruption, preterm labor, and fetal compromise.

424
00:52:18,004 --> 00:52:26,516
Bradycardia is common during or following ECV, but emergent cesarean rates are ≤0.5 percent.

425
00:52:26,466 --> 00:52:39,366
Uterine rupture, fetomaternal hemorrhage, alloimmunization, amnionic fluid embolism, and maternal or fetal deaths are rare (Grootscholten, 2008; Rodgers, 2017).

426
00:52:39,366 --> 00:52:50,916
Overall, compared with expectant management, perinatal morbidity and mortality rates are not greater with ECV (Krueger, 2018; Son, 2018).

427
00:52:50,916 --> 00:53:00,954
Even after successful ECV, several reports suggest that the cesarean delivery rate does not completely revert to the baseline for vertex presentations.

428
00:53:00,954 --> 00:53:16,741
Specifically, dystocia, malpresentation, and nonreassuring fetal heart patterns may be more common in these fetuses who have undergone successful ECV (Chan, 2004; de Hundt, 2014; Vézina, 2004).

429
00:53:16,741 --> 00:53:18,904
# Technique.

430
00:53:18,904 --> 00:53:26,829
ECV should be carried out in an area that has ready access to a facility equipped to perform emergency cesarean delivery.

431
00:53:26,829 --> 00:53:32,141
Because of the risk for surgical intervention, intravenous access is obtained.

432
00:53:32,141 --> 00:53:39,166
Patients also abstain from eating for 6 hours, but clear liquids can be consumed up to 2 hours prior.

433
00:53:39,166 --> 00:53:55,129
Sonographic examination is performed to confirm nonvertex presentation, document amnionic fluid volume adequacy, exclude obvious fetal anomalies if not done previously, and identify placental location and fetal spine orientation.

434
00:53:55,129 --> 00:54:01,054
Preprocedural external monitoring is performed to document fetal heart rate reactivity.

435
00:54:01,054 --> 00:54:05,929
Anti-D immune globulin is given to Rh D–negative women.

436
00:54:05,929 --> 00:54:13,554
Tocolysis and regional analgesia may be elected, and rationale for these is provided in subsequent sections.

437
00:54:13,554 --> 00:54:22,316
The woman is placed in left lateral tilt to aid uteroplacental perfusion, and Trendelenburg positioning helps during elevation of the breech.

438
00:54:22,316 --> 00:54:27,716
During the procedure, we prefer to monitor fetal heart motion sonographically.

439
00:54:27,716 --> 00:54:34,591
An abundant abdominal coating of ultrasound gel permits this and also minimizes painful skin friction .

440
00:54:34,591 --> 00:54:38,516
A forward roll of the fetus usually is attempted first.

441
00:54:38,516 --> 00:54:43,366
One or two providers may participate, and one hand grasps the head.

442
00:54:43,366 --> 00:54:48,766
The fetal buttocks are then elevated from the maternal pelvis and displaced laterally .

443
00:54:48,766 --> 00:54:55,279
These are then gently guided toward the fundus, while the head is simultaneously directed toward the pelvis.

444
00:54:55,279 --> 00:54:59,916
If the forward roll is unsuccessful, a backward flip is attempted.

445
00:54:59,916 --> 00:55:08,929
ECV attempts are discontinued for excessive discomfort, for persistently abnormal fetal heart rate, or after multiple failed attempts.

446
00:55:08,929 --> 00:55:11,804
Failure is not always absolute.

447
00:55:11,804 --> 00:55:24,604
Ben-Meir and colleagues (2007) reported a spontaneous version rate of 7 percent among 226 failed versions—2 percent among nulliparas and 13 percent among multiparas.

448
00:55:24,604 --> 00:55:27,504
Figure 28-13.

449
00:55:27,504 --> 00:55:30,066
External cephalic version.

450
00:55:30,066 --> 00:55:35,804
With an attempted forward roll, clockwise pressure is exerted against the fetal poles.

451
00:55:35,804 --> 00:55:42,304
If ECV is successful, a nonstress test is repeated until a normal test result is obtained.

452
00:55:42,304 --> 00:55:49,754
A transient abnormal fetal heart rate tracing during or after ECV complicates 6 to 9 percent of cases.

453
00:55:49,754 --> 00:55:56,791
This should prompt a traditional resuscitative response with intravenous fluids, oxygen, and lateral tilt.

454
00:55:56,791 --> 00:56:08,816
Rates of abnormal tracings prompting immediate cesarean delivery range from only 0.2 to 0.4 percent (Collaris, 2004; Grootscholten, 2008).

455
00:56:08,816 --> 00:56:19,029
Once resolved, Kuppens and coworkers (2017) found that a transient abnormal tracing did not predict later fetal distress in subsequent labor.

456
00:56:19,029 --> 00:56:27,029
If ECV is completed before 39 weeks’ gestation, awaiting spontaneous labor and fetal maturity is preferred.

457
00:56:27,029 --> 00:56:36,816
In some studies, immediate labor induction is linked to higher cesarean delivery rates (Burgos, 2015; Kuppens, 2013).

458
00:56:36,816 --> 00:56:43,804
Tocolysis By relaxing the uterus prior to an ECV attempt, tocolysis raises success rates.

459
00:56:43,804 --> 00:56:49,166
Most data support the use of the betamimetics terbutaline and ritodrine for this .

460
00:56:49,166 --> 00:57:02,404
In one such trial, Fernandez and coworkers (1996) reported that the success rate with subcutaneous terbutaline—52 percent— was significantly higher than without—27 percent.

461
00:57:02,404 --> 00:57:11,791
Our policy at Parkland Hospital is to administer 250 μg of terbutaline subcutaneously to most women before attempted ECV.

462
00:57:11,791 --> 00:57:18,041
When maternal tachycardia—a known side effect of terbutaline—is noted, the attempt is

463
00:57:18,196 --> 00:57:19,708
begun.

464
00:57:19,658 --> 00:57:30,708
Terbutaline readily crosses the placenta, and mild elevation of fetal heart rate baseline also is possible (Ingemarsson, 1981; Roth, 1990).

465
00:57:30,708 --> 00:57:58,371
Data are limited and in some cases are nonsupportive for other agents that include calcium-channel blockers, such as nifedipine; nitric oxide donors, such as nitroglycerin; the oxytocin-receptor antagonist atosiban; and another betamimetic salbutamol (Burgos, 2010; Cluver, 2015; Hilton, 2009; Levin, 2019; Vani, 2009; Wilcox, 2011).

466
00:57:58,371 --> 00:58:12,433
Conduction Analgesia Epidural analgesia coupled with tocolysis has been reported to raise ECV success rates compared with tocolysis alone (Goetzinger, 2011; MagroMalosso, 2016).

467
00:58:12,433 --> 00:58:23,583
Moreover, rates of complications that include fetal heart rate aberrations, emergency cesarean delivery, or placental abruption are not greater with regional analgesia.

468
00:58:23,583 --> 00:58:33,196
In trials, spinal and epidural have both shown success and are reasonable options (Khaw, 2015; Weiniger, 2010).

469
00:58:33,196 --> 00:58:39,946
Although uncommon, risks specific to regional analgesia, such as spinal headache, are possible.

470
00:58:39,946 --> 00:58:45,221
Currently, the superior technique and best drugs to administer are unclear.

471
00:58:45,221 --> 00:59:01,158
In contrast, from limited data, intravenous sedation or nitrous oxide does not appear to improve ECV success (Burgos, 2016; Dochez, 2019; Khaw, 2015; Straube, 2021).

472
00:59:01,158 --> 00:59:03,558
# Moxibustion.

473
00:59:03,558 --> 00:59:13,883
This is a traditional Chinese medicine technique that burns a cigarette-shaped stick of ground Artemisia vulgaris—which is also known as mugwort or in Japanese as moxa.

474
00:59:13,883 --> 00:59:26,308
At the BL 67 acupuncture point, the stick is directly placed against the skin or indirectly heats an acupuncture needle at the site to increase fetal movement and promote spontaneous breech version .

475
00:59:26,308 --> 00:59:34,033
It is performed usually between 33 and 36 weeks’ gestation to permit a trial of ECV if not successful.

476
00:59:34,033 --> 00:59:47,696
Results from randomized controlled studies are conflicting (Bue, 2016; Coulon, 2014; Coyle, 2012; Sananes, 2016; Vas, 2013).

